  "full_response": "Software testing is a critical phase in the software development lifecycle (SDLC) that ensures the quality, functionality, and reliability of a product. Here are the main types of software testing, along with when and why each should be used:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Functional Testing**\n**Purpose**: Verify that the software functions as intended according to requirements.\n**When to use**:\n- After development to validate core features.\n- Before major releases or updates.\n- When new features are added or existing ones are modified.\n\n**Subtypes**:\n- **Unit Testing**: Tests individual components (e.g., functions, methods) in isolation. Used during development (often by developers).\n- **Integration Testing**: Tests interactions between modules/components. Used after unit testing to ensure combined parts work together.\n- **System Testing**: Tests the complete system against requirements. Used before user acceptance testing (UAT).\n- **Acceptance Testing (UAT)**: Validates the system against business/user requirements. Used before deployment (often by end-users or clients).\n- **Regression Testing**: Re-tests the software after changes to ensure no new bugs were introduced. Used after bug fixes or updates.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Non-Functional Testing**\n**Purpose**: Evaluate non-functional aspects like performance, usability, and security.\n**When to use**:\n- After functional testing is complete.\n- Before deployment to ensure scalability, security, and user experience.\n- Periodically for performance-critical applications (e.g., load testing during peak usage).\n\n**Subtypes**:\n- **Performance Testing**: Checks speed, scalability, and stability under load.\n  - *Load Testing*: Tests behavior under expected user loads.\n  - *Stress Testing*: Tests limits by pushing beyond normal capacity.\n  - *Endurance Testing*: Checks performance over extended periods.\n- **Usability Testing**: Evaluates user-friendliness and accessibility. Used during design and prototyping.\n- **Security Testing**: Identifies vulnerabilities (e.g., SQL injection, XSS). Used before deployment and periodically.\n- **Compatibility Testing**: Ensures the software works across devices, browsers, or OS versions. Used before release.\n- **Reliability Testing**: Checks if the software performs consistently over time.\n- **Localization/Internationalization Testing**: Validates language, cultural, and regional adaptations.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Maintenance Testing**\n**Purpose**: Ensures the software remains functional after changes (e.g., patches, updates).\n**When to use**:\n- After bug fixes, enhancements, or environment changes",
